  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 931 PM MDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nWINDY AND DRIER THROUGH FRIDAY: \n\nWindy conditions are expected through Friday, with the gusts around \n25 to 35 mph. This is due to an increased pressure gradient and \nperiods of stronger winds aloft as the trough moves deeper into the \nWest. Drier air moves in today which will keep precipitation chances \npretty low through the rest of the workweek. However, some isolated \nafternoon convection is possible over the higher terrain tomorrow \nand Friday. \n\nRETURN OF MOISTURE THIS WEEKEND: \n\nThis weekend a low pressure will be anchored over the Pacific \nNorthwest with high pressure over the Southern Plains. This will \nresult in southwest flow aloft, which will advect moisture back into \nthe area. Each day we should see an increase in showers and storms. \nSunday and Monday seem to be the peak moisture. Also, the models are \nindicating the potential for shortwaves rotating around the main \nlow over the Pacific Northwest. If that happens increased forcing \ncould help with precipitation development. Given PWATs over 200 \npercent of normal there will be a chance for heavy rainfall. That \npotential will be dictated by whether there is enough clearing for \ninstability. The ground is pretty saturated in spots so it might not \ntake much to trigger flooding and debris flows. Where the models \nbegin to disagree is if the moisture plume sticks around or if the \nshortwave activity pushes the moisture plume to our southeast. \nTherefore confidence in the forecast early next week is not high \nattm. Temperatures during this period should end up near normal to \nbelow normal especially with the cloud cover and rain.",
